<div class="manifesto prose">
	<p>
	We don't study mathematics to memorise formulas, but to understand why they must be true.
	</p>

	<p>
	We don't learn to program to command machines, but to understand what can be computed, and what cannot.
	</p>

	<p>
	We don't trust an idea because of who said it, but because we tried to break it and failed.
	</p>

	<p>
	We don't accept "it works" as an answer, but keep asking how, why, and at what cost.
	</p>

	<p>
	We don't fear being wrong, because every mistake is a data point and every correction is progress we can measure.
	</p>

	<p>
	We don't collect certificates, but skills we can demonstrate on paper, at a keyboard, and under pressure.
	</p>

	<p>
	We don't pretend knowledge costs nothing. It is built by those who discover it, carried by those who teach it, and earned by those who learn it. Students need a source they can rely on, and teachers need a livelihood that lets them keep teaching. An academy that serves only one of them serves neither.
	</p>

	<p>
	We don't teach for applause or learn for grades, but to know we are capable of understanding the world, and of adding to it.
	</p>

	<p>
	Every lecture is a hypothesis, every exercise an experiment, every exam a measurement. We feed our starving minds with evidence, not opinion.
	</p>
</div>

